course: technology
lesson: 4
title: Bottles, cans and recycling
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A plastic drinks bottle starts life as a little plastic tube about the size of your finger, which is heated and blown up like a balloon inside a bottle-shaped mould in about a second.
explain: A drinks can starts as a flat disc of aluminium punched into a cup and stretched tall, and a glass bottle starts as a glowing blob of melted sand and old glass blown into a mould.
explain: On a bottling line, empty bottles rush along like runners in a relay: rinsed, filled to the same height, capped and labelled, tens of thousands an hour.
explain: When you drop a can in the recycling, it rides belts in a sorting plant where magnets, air jets and cameras split it from paper, plastic and steel, and it can be back on a shop shelf as a new can in about two months.
explain: Batteries thrown in with the recycling can start fires in lorries and plants, so they go in special battery bins instead.
fact: A plastic bottle is blown from a preform, a small finger-sized plastic tube, heated and blown up with air inside a bottle-shaped mould.
fact: Plastic dropped as litter can last hundreds of years, so plastic bottles should go in the recycling.
fact: A drinks can is punched from a disc of aluminium into a cup and stretched up through rings into a tall thin can; the top is fixed on after filling, and the domed bottom makes the can strong against the gas pressure inside.
fact: Aluminium cans can be recycled again and again and can be back on a shop shelf in about two months.
fact: Glass is made by melting sand, soda ash and limestone with lots of recycled glass in a furnace that runs day and night for years, and machines blow blobs of hot glass into moulds; broken glass cuts, so tell an adult instead of picking it up.
fact: Fizzy drinks have carbon dioxide gas pushed in under pressure; cold drinks hold more gas than warm ones, so warm drinks go flat faster.
fact: A shaken fizzy bottle can spray hard when opened, so never point one at anyone's face.
fact: A bottling line rinses, fills, caps and labels bottles one after another, and fast lines fill tens of thousands of bottles an hour.
fact: A ring-pull is a little lever fixed to the lid; stay-on tabs were invented in the 1970s because old pull-off tabs littered beaches; small caps are a choking danger for babies.
fact: A juice carton is made of layers of paper, plastic and foil thinner than a hair, which keep light and air out, so it cannot be recycled like plain paper with the newspapers.
fact: In a sorting plant magnets pull out steel, a spinning magnet pushes aluminium cans off the belt even though aluminium is not magnetic, and air jets with cameras pick out the plastics.
fact: Refill fountains push mains water through a filter and a cooler, and in many countries tap water is tested more often than bottled water.
fact: Bottle labels are printed on long rolls and glued on, or a plastic tube called a shrink sleeve is pulled over the bottle and shrunk tight in a hot tunnel.
exercise: How is a plastic drinks bottle made? | A small plastic tube is heated and blown up inside a mould | Melted plastic is poured into a bottle shape like jelly | It is carved out of a solid plastic block | It is rolled up from a flat plastic sheet and glued down the side
exercise: How big is a preform before it is blown up? | About the size of a finger | About the size of a bucket | Just as big as the finished bottle | As small as a grain of salt
exercise: Why should plastic bottles go in the recycling and not on the ground? | Dropped plastic can last hundreds of years | Plastic melts into the soil after a few days of rain | Plastic bottles slowly turn into glass when left outside | Plastic rots away in a week and makes the street smell bad
exercise: What is a drinks can made from? | A disc of aluminium punched and stretched tall | A thin sheet of tin rolled into a tube and glued shut | Thick cardboard coated in layers of shiny metal paint | Copper wire woven tightly together and then pressed flat
exercise: Why is the bottom of a can shaped like a dome? | The dome makes it stronger against the gas pressure inside | So the can wobbles and is fun to spin | To make the can hold a little less drink | So the cans can only be stacked upside down on the shop shelves
exercise: How soon can a recycled can be back on a shop shelf? | In about two months | In about two hundred years | Never, because cans cannot be recycled | In about ten years
exercise: What is glass made from? | Sand, soda ash and limestone, plus old glass | Melted plastic mixed with salt | Water frozen very hard | Sugar boiled for many days
exercise: What gas makes a fizzy drink fizz? | Carbon dioxide pushed in under pressure | Oxygen taken from the air | Bubbles of washing-up soap | Steam from boiling the drink
exercise: Why does a warm fizzy drink go flat faster? | Warm drinks hold less gas than cold ones | Warm drinks contain more sugar | The cap melts a little in the warm | Warm drinks suck in extra gas from the air
exercise: What does a bottling line do? | Rinses, fills, caps and labels bottles one after another | Grows the fruit on the farm and then squeezes it to make juice | Drinks up any bottles left over | Sorts rubbish into different bins
exercise: Why do ring-pulls stay on cans now? | Old pull-off tabs littered beaches, so stay-on tabs were invented | Ring-pulls can be swapped for money | The can would leak without the tab on | Shoppers asked for something to play with
exercise: Why are small bottle caps a danger for babies? | Babies can choke on them | Caps are poisonous to touch | Caps can explode in the sun | Caps carry electricity
exercise: What is a juice carton made of? | Layers of paper, plastic and very thin foil | Only thick brown paper | Only clear plastic | Wax and string
exercise: How does a sorting plant pull steel cans out? | With magnets | With fishing nets | With hot air blowers | With sticky rollers
exercise: How can aluminium cans jump off the belt when aluminium is not magnetic? | A spinning magnet pushes them away | They stick to a roller covered in glue | A worker flicks every one off by hand | They float up with the warm air
exercise: What does a water refill fountain do? | Filters and cools mains water to fill your bottle | Makes fizzy drinks out of the rainwater on the roof | Sells water to you in brand-new plastic bottles each time | Turns old empty bottles back into water you can drink
exercise: How is a shrink-sleeve label put on a bottle? | A plastic tube shrinks tight round the bottle in a hot tunnel | It is painted on with a brush | It is sewn on by a machine | It is stuck on with tape by hand
test: «chucked the old batteries in the recycling, it's all recycling innit» What is right? | No, batteries can start fires in lorries and plants; use battery bins | Yes, batteries go in with cans and paper | Batteries should go down the drain | Dead batteries are safe in any bin at all, as they have no power left
test: «shook my mate's coke, gonna open it at his face lol» What is right? | Never point a shaken bottle at a face, it can spray hard | Fine, it is only a bit of sweet drink and it washes off easily | Shaking takes all the fizz out first | Nothing happens if you open it slowly at a face
test: «bottled water's way cleaner than tap, everyone knows» What is right? | In many countries tap water is tested more often than bottled water | Tap water is never tested by anyone | Bottled water always comes from a clean mountain spring, so it is purer | Tap water is always dirty water
test: «smashed a jar on the kitchen floor, gonna grab the bits quick» What should you do? | Keep away from the bits and tell an adult | Pick up all the pieces fast with your bare hands | Sweep them away quickly with your bare feet | Hide the broken bits under the rug so nobody sees
test: What would happen if a cola can had a thin flat bottom instead of a dome? | It could bulge from the gas pressure inside | It would hold twice as much cola as a normal can | The cola inside it would taste a lot sweeter | It would stay fizzy for ever, even after opening
test: «juice carton goes in with the newspapers yeah, it's cardboard» What is right? | No, it also has plastic and foil layers, so it needs special recycling | Yes, it is only paper inside | It goes in the bottle bank with glass | No carton can ever be recycled
test: In a cartoon a recycling lorry tips everything into one hole and new cans pop straight out. What happens first in real life? | Belts, magnets, air jets, cameras and people sort the materials | Everything is melted together at once | The recycling is buried in the ground | The lorry squeezes all the rubbish together into brand-new cans on the way
test: A crate holds 4 rows of 6 bottles. How many bottles are in 2 crates? | 48 | 24 | 20 | 34
test: «my can's ring-pull won't come off, it's broke» What is the real story? | Stay-on tabs are meant to stay; old ones came off and littered beaches | All ring-pulls are meant to come off | The tab comes off only if you win a prize | Cans never had ring-pulls before
test: A pack has 6 cans. Leo drinks 2, no wait, 3, and his sister drinks 1. How many cans are left? | 2 | 3 | 4 | 10
test: «leave the cola in the sun, it'll get extra fizzy» What is right? | It goes flat faster, because warm drinks hold less gas | The sun makes extra gas in the drink, so it gets much fizzier | The sun turns the cola into plain water | The sun makes no difference at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manufacture of a plastic drinks bottle? | A heated preform is inflated with air within a mould shaped as the bottle | Molten plastic is poured into a mould and permitted to set as a solid block | The bottle is carved from a single block of plastic | The bottle is assembled from flat plastic panels united by glue
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a warm carbonated drink loses its effervescence more rapidly than a cold one. | A warm liquid retains less dissolved gas than a cold liquid | A warm liquid contains a greater quantity of sugar than a cold one | The container ceases to be sealed when warmed | Warmth converts the gas into water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of batteries. | They are to be deposited in designated battery bins, since they may cause fires in recycling vehicles | They are to be placed with paper and cardboard for recycling | They are to be disposed of via the household drain | They present no hazard whatsoever in any receptacle
exam: Determine the aggregate number of bottles contained in three crates, it being given that each crate holds twenty bottles. | Sixty | Twenty-three | Forty | Ninety
